{"question_id": "th-004", "category": "thesis", "question": "What investable thesis do Meridian Tech Daily and Lumina funding news articulate about agent infrastructure?", "expected_answer_keywords": ["inference cost", "sub-$1", "agent"], "forbidden_keywords": [], "expected_doc_keys": ["lumina-small-model-thesis-news", "lumina-inference-funding"], "expected_abstain": false, "notes": "News synthesis plus funding strategic rationale.", "answer": "Both Meridian Tech Daily and Lumina's funding announcements articulate an investable thesis that the **collapse of inference costs for small (sub-12B) models** will serve as the primary catalyst for widespread **agentic product adoption and infrastructure scaling**. They argue that achieving sub-$1 per million token serving economics\u2014driven by quantization techniques, edge hardware optimization, and internal efficiency gains\u2014fundamentally reshapes agent economics by making autonomous workflows commercially viable at scale. To capitalize on this thesis, Lumina secured $180 million in Series C funding specifically to drive further API unit-cost reductions, while Meridian's analysis validates that these cost declines are already transforming the economic feasibility of deploying agent infrastructure.", "abstained": false, "cited_doc_keys": ["lumina-inference-funding", "lumina-small-model-thesis-news"], "retrieved_doc_keys": ["lumina-api-pricing-feb-2026", "lumina-api-pricing-nov-2025", "lumina-inference-funding", "lumina-small-model-thesis-news", "lumina-spark-2-0-release"], "latency_s": 32.58742456138134, "tokens_used": 3581, "answer_correctness": 1.0, "forbidden_violation": false, "evidence_recall_at_k": 1.0, "citation_precision": 1.0, "citation_faithfulness": true, "temporal_correctness": null, "supersession_correctness": null, "abstention_accuracy": true}
